#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
verify-jurisdiction — the Onboarding Probe (OPEN-222, PLAN-push-button-onboarding.md §4.4).

Answers "what will this state cost?" in ~30 minutes, before anyone commits to onboarding it,
by running gates A-I against the real scraper toolchain and the local OpenStates DB. Read-only
by design (see "Safety" below) -- it never writes to a production database, never edits scraper
code, never files tickets, and never touches ddp-sync/ddp-broker-py/ddp-next/votebot.

Depends on OPEN-221 (jurisdictions.yaml / validate_jurisdictions.py / jurisdiction-field.sh),
this branch's own base. Uses jurisdiction-field.sh's manifest only to read an existing entry's
recorded values for context (e.g. api_keys) when one exists -- a brand-new state has no manifest
entry yet, which is fine, every gate below works from the live scraper/DB, not the manifest.

Gates (PLAN-push-button-onboarding.md §4.4's table, ticket OPEN-222's restatement of it):
    A (blocking)  get_session_list() >= 1 session; check_session_list() passes; active sessions
    B (blocking)  scraper module shape: votes scraper? start= supported? module-level
                  settings/RPM? required API keys present in the environment?
    C (blocking)  CONVERSION_FUNCTIONS[<st>] covers every media_type actually present upstream
    D (blocking)  bounded, timed dry scrape; nonzero objects; per-bill latency; block signatures
    E (advisory)  motion_classification.yaml has a block for this state? sample motion texts
    F (advisory)  version walk direction, informational only (OPEN-34: quality.walk_direction
                  itself is obsolete -- this gate reports a finding, never writes that field)
    G (advisory)  people data present; broker fixture district counts (best-effort); session
                  code shape vs. the broker's `^(\\d{3}|\\d{4}|\\d{4}[a-zA-Z]+)$` regex
    H (blocking)  BillVersionLink rows exist and aren't a stale snapshot; with --import, the
                  state's session rows are created the way a real import creates them and listed
    I (blocking)  no vote dedupe_key repeats across the sessions sampled (OPEN-397's collision)

Output: a scored, classified (green/yellow/red) markdown report written to
notes/<st>-onboarding-probe-<date>.md (PLAN §5 Stage 0's exact naming convention).

Safety (judgment calls made explicit, since the plan text doesn't spell out DB-write mechanics):
  - Gates C and D never touch a database. They run a bounded, capped dry scrape (like this
    repo's existing test-mi-scrape-sample.py, generalized to any jurisdiction) that writes
    scraped JSON to an ISOLATED scratch subdirectory -- never the real `_data/<st>` `run-scrape.sh`
    and a live production scrape use -- and gate C derives extractor coverage from that JSON, not
    from a live-API sample (both are named as acceptable in the plan; JSON is what a plain
    dry-scrape run already produces, no extra network budget spent).
  - Gate D respects the existing same-jurisdiction scrape lock (`/tmp/ddp-openstates-scrape-locks/
    $STATE`, OPEN-154) as a courtesy check before scraping, and always writes to its own scratch
    subdirectory regardless, so it cannot collide with a concurrent real scrape's data even if the
    lock has already been released mid-check.
  - Gate H is READ-ONLY by default: it queries whatever BillVersionLink rows already exist in the
    configured DATABASE_URL. Passing --import additionally imports the gate D dry-scrape's bounded
    JSON sample (a real write) -- and that is refused unless DATABASE_URL is recognizable as a
    non-production database (name ends in _dev/_test, or --force-import is passed explicitly).
    This is the one place this script could write to a database at all, and it defaults to off.

Usage:
    verify-jurisdiction <st> [--session ID] [--sample-bills N] [--max-objects M] [--import] [--notes-dir DIR]
    verify-jurisdiction <st> --fargate [--sample-bills N] [--max-objects M]   (OPEN-458: as a Fargate task, no database)
    verify-jurisdiction <st> --from-dir DIR [--import]          (OPEN-458: finish a Fargate run here)

--fargate runs gates A-D, E, F and I on a cloud IP and uploads report.md, result.json and the bounded
sample under $MEMORY_PREFIX/probe/<st>/<run id>/ in $MEMORY_BUCKET; G and H are skipped (no database
there), so a YELLOW is provisional and a RED is final. It refuses --import, Michigan, and a "prod"
MEMORY_PREFIX. --from-dir takes gates A, B and D from that run's result.json and runs the rest here
against the downloaded sample. RUNBOOK.md "Running the onboarding probe on Fargate" has the commands.

Must be run with this repo's environment active (source activate.sh or activate-dev.sh first) --
same requirement as every other script in this repo (see PRIMITIVES.md).
"""

